Homework 2 Summary

The goal of Homework 2 was to build on the first version of Zag Farkle by letting the player
continue their turn through multiple rolls. The updated program asks for a player name,
displays an introduction banner, and lets the player choose dice for a meld using the same
A-F menu from Homework 1. The player can bank their points, reroll the dice that have not
scored, or quit and see their current total. If a roll is a Farkle, the turn ends and all points
earned during that turn are lost.

I kept the original classes and added a Player class. Farkle contains the short main method
that starts the game. FarkleGame handles input and controls the turn and Die represents
one six-sided die. Hand stores the current roll and tracks which dice are selected. Meld
calculates the score using the scoring rules from Homework 1. Player stores the name and
accumulated points. 

One important change was allowing Hand to contain anywhere from one to six dice. After a
valid meld, the program adds its points to the running total and creates a new hand with the
correct number of leftover dice. The previous meld's dice are no longer available for selection. 
This keeps points from separate rolls from being counted again or combined into a new triple. If
every remaining die is used in a scoring combo, the program announces a hot hand and allows six 
new dice on the next roll.

A major design issue was distinguishing selected dice from dice that actually score. Meld
handles this by counting the dice used in scoring combos. Hand subtracts that count from
the current roll size to decide how many dice to roll next. This also prevents the program
from announcing a hot hand just because every die was selected. Another issue was keeping
the earlier meld points while still resetting the whole turn to zero after a Farkle. Storing the
accumulated points in Player keeps that separate from the current meld's score.

If I had more time, I would add more complete turn tests for several rerolls. Also, the current 
version focuses on one player's turn, but the separate Player and game classes provide a 
starting point for adding multiple players and rounds later.
